annotations.description:
{{ if $values }}Environment: ${ALERT_ENVIRONMENT}
System key: {{ .Labels.key }}
Destination: {{ .Labels.destination }}
Largest response in the last 10 minutes: {{ humanize1024 .Values.LARGEST.Value }}B ({{ printf "%.0f" .Values.LARGEST.Value }} bytes), {{ humanizePercentage .Values.SHARE.Value }} of the cap
The key's current cap: {{ humanize1024 .Values.CAP.Value }}B ({{ printf "%.0f" .Values.CAP.Value }} bytes)

First check that the response is legitimate: a vendor answering a real request, not a runaway or a decompression bomb (a small compressed body that decompresses past the cap). The outbound-guard skill has the queries. If it is legitimate, raise this key's cap. It takes effect on the key's next call, with no deploy:

boxel realm-server keys set-response-cap --realm-server ${REALM_SERVER_URL} --destination {{ reReplaceAll "^system:" "" .Labels.key }} {{ printf "%.0f" .Values.SUGGESTED.Value }}

The suggested value is twice the current cap. It is never more than 1 GiB (1073741824 bytes), the largest cap the command accepts. A key already at 1 GiB can't go higher: a legitimate response that size belongs on a file-transfer path, not a system key's forward. Pick another from the sizes on the Keys dashboard's Response size cap row.{{ else }}Environment: ${ALERT_ENVIRONMENT}

Grafana could not run this rule's query. This is the rule's DatasourceError alert, and its Error annotation gives the reason. It shows nothing about system keys' response sizes. The query reads Loki: check the Loki task and its context canceled and context deadline exceeded errors.{{ end }}

